<article class="page-body">
  <div class="container">
    <h1>Kiavi Deal Calculator</h1>
    <p class="lede">Enter three numbers from your deal. The calculator applies Kiavi's typical loan ceilings (95% loan-to-cost, 80% of ARV), the classic 70% rule, and a realistic cost stack to show your max loan, cash to close, and projected profit.</p>

    <div class="calc" id="calc">
      <label for="purchase">Purchase price ($)</label>
      <input type="number" id="purchase" value="200000" min="0" step="1000">
      <label for="rehab">Rehab budget ($)</label>
      <input type="number" id="rehab" value="50000" min="0" step="1000">
      <label for="arv">After-repair value — ARV ($)</label>
      <input type="number" id="arv" value="330000" min="0" step="1000">
      <label for="months">Expected hold (months)</label>
      <input type="number" id="months" value="8" min="1" max="24" step="1">
      <label for="rate">Interest rate (%)</label>
      <input type="number" id="rate" value="10" min="0" max="20" step="0.125">

      <div class="calc-results" id="results" aria-live="polite"></div>
      <p class="fineprint">Estimates only, using typical Kiavi maximums (95% LTC / 80% ARV), 2 origination points, ~8% selling costs, and interest on the full loan for the hold period. Your actual leverage and pricing depend on experience, credit, and the property — <a href="{{ site.affiliateLink }}" rel="sponsored noopener" target="_blank">get a real quote</a> (soft pull, minutes).</p>
    </div>

    <h2>How to read the results</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Max Kiavi loan</strong> — the lower of 95% of your total cost and 80% of ARV. Whichever ceiling binds tells you what kind of deal you have: ARV-bound deals are thin.</li>
      <li><strong>Cash to close</strong> — your cost gap plus points. Rehab is financed but drawn as reimbursement, so keep reserves for the first stage of work (<a href="/kiavi-fix-and-flip/">how draws work</a>).</li>
      <li><strong>70% rule max offer</strong> — the old-school screen: 70% of ARV minus rehab. If your purchase price is above it, the calculator flags it; plenty of good deals break the 70% rule in expensive markets, but know you're breaking it.</li>
      <li><strong>Projected profit</strong> — ARV minus every cost in the stack. If this number doesn't survive a 10% ARV haircut, pass.</li>
    </ul>

    {% include "partials/cta.njk" %}

    <h2>Pair it with Kiavi's ARV estimator</h2>
    <p>Your ARV input is the whole game. Kiavi's free <a href="/faqs/what-is-kiavi-arv-estimator/">ARV estimator</a> predicts post-renovation value from its underwriting models — use it to sanity-check your comps before you trust the profit number here. Then check the <a href="/kiavi-requirements/">requirements</a> and <a href="/kiavi-rates/">current rates</a>.</p>
